1. Initial Access – How attackers get in
Phishing – Emails with malicious links or attachments (T1566)

Brute Force / Password Spraying – Guessing passwords (T1110)

Exploit Public-Facing Application – Hacking web apps (T1190) – SQL Injection belongs here

Drive‑by Compromise – Malicious website exploits browser (T1189)

2. Execution – How they run their code
PowerShell – Running malicious scripts (T1059.001)

Command and Scripting Interpreter – Using cmd, bash, etc. (T1059)

User Execution – Tricking user into running a file (T1204) – Malicious macros

Scheduled Task/Job – Creating tasks to run code (T1053)

3. Persistence – How they stay in
Registry Run Keys – Adding startup entries (T1547.001)

Scheduled Tasks – Re‑running malware (T1053.005)

Create Account – Adding a backdoor user (T1136)

4. Privilege Escalation – How they get more rights
Exploitation for Privilege Escalation – Using a bug (T1068)

Access Token Manipulation – Impersonating tokens (T1134)

UAC Bypass – Bypassing Windows User Account Control (T1548.002)

5. Defense Evasion – How they hide
Disable Security Tools – Turning off antivirus (T1562.001)

Process Injection – Hiding code in legitimate processes (T1055)

Obfuscated Files or Information – Encoded PowerShell, packed malware (T1027)

Clear Windows Event Logs – wevtutil cl (T1070.001)

6. Credential Access – How they steal passwords
Credential Dumping – Mimikatz, LSASS access (T1003)

Keylogging – Recording keystrokes (T1056.001)

Network Sniffing – Capturing plaintext passwords (T1040)

7. Lateral Movement – How they spread
Pass‑the‑Hash – Using password hashes to authenticate (T1550.002)

Remote Desktop Protocol (RDP) – Brute‑force or legitimate use (T1021.001)

SMB/Windows Admin Shares – Moving via \admin$ (T1021.002)

8. Command and Control (C2) – How they talk to home base
Beaconing – Regular check‑ins to external IP (TA0011)

DNS Tunneling – Hiding data in DNS queries (T1572)

Web Protocols – HTTP/HTTPS C2 (T1071.001)

9. Exfiltration – How they steal data
Data Compression – Zipping before sending (T1560)

Exfiltration Over C2 Channel – Stealing via the command channel (T1041)

Automated Exfiltration – Tools like rclone (T1020)

10. Impact – The final damage
Ransomware – Encrypting files (T1486)

Data Destruction – Deleting or corrupting data (T1485)

Denial of Service (DoS) – Flooding services (T1498)

